WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:01.534
A former Twitter employee

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.969
who gave evidence to the January

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:03.536
six select

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.605
committee is speaking out publicly.

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:06.673
The whistleblower says

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.408
the committee missed an opportunity

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:09.676
to hold social media

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:10.877
companies accountable

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:11.811
for their roles

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:13.413
in the Capitol insurrection.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.147
CNN's Donie

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:16.683
O'Sullivan has this exclusive report.

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:20.020
I do fear for the future

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.755
and what it may hold.

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:22.789
You think there could be

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:24.991
another January six in this country?

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:25.825
Yes.

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:27.193
Anika Collier Navaroli,

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:28.128
is a former Twitter

00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:29.929
employee, turned whistleblower

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:30.597
who testified

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:31.865
before the January six committee

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:33.366
initially anonymously.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.101
A lot of the locks and motives

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:36.036
boxed in by

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:36.903
those tweets

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:39.039
were in response to Donald Trump

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:40.774
Now she is speaking exclusively

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:42.976
to CNN in her first television interview.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.577
And I think it's really important

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:45.612
for these findings

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:47.180
from the committee about the roles

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.915
that social media played

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:51.418
with in January six come to light.

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:53.887
Navaroli says she can't talk specifics

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:55.555
about her time at Twitter publicly,

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:56.222
but she shared

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:57.390
Eye-opening details

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.291
in depositions

00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:00.226
with the January six committee.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.061
One example,

00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:01.561
as Trump

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.530
supporters gathered in Washington, D.C.,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.298
on the eve of the Capitol attack

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:06.533
Navaroli and her colleagues

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.101
warned management at Twitter

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:08.835
there might be

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.870
someone getting shot tomorrow.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.072
According to transcripts

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:13.139
of her deposition.

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:14.374
But Twitter leadership

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:15.742
refused to take action.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:16.543
Attorney Alexis Ronickher

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.078
spoke to CNN on behalf

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:19.712
of a second Twitter whistleblower

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.214
who is remaining anonymous.

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:22.348
Wasn't actually

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:24.884
until the doors of the Capitol

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.219
were being breached

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:28.321
that Twitter leadership

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:29.389
started taking action.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:29.923
At that point,

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:30.890
it was too little, too late.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.859
The real harm and violence had happened

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:36.362
How did you

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.231
feel as an American

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:40.300
just seeing this happen? Terrified.

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:43.403
It was horrifying.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.874
To experience

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.809
political violence

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:52.779
happen within our country

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.981
at such a grand scale.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:56.316
Jacob Glick was a lawyer

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:57.450
for the January six committee

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.885
who deposed narrowly.

00:01:58.885 --> 00:01:59.919
She described

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.222
employees, including herself,

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:05.492
coming forward to warn

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:07.727
their supervisors in Amicus telling.

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.262
They were denied over

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:12.332
and over and over and over and who knows

00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:14.000
what could have been avoided.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.603
If they had listened to her

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:17.704
and her colleagues sooner.

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.139
But Navaroli is not happy

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:19.939
with the January six

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:21.541
committee's final report.

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:22.142
Social media

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:23.309
companies are mentioned

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.911
hundreds of times

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.646
within the final report.

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:28.481
However, their role

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:31.484
or their responsibility within that day

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:32.619
and the events of that day

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:34.287
and the violence that occurred

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:35.822
has not been fully laid out.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:37.690
The committee had a so-called purple team

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.659
dedicated to looking into social media

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:40.760
and extremism.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:41.461
CNN obtained

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:43.696
a copy of an unpublished draft document

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:44.697
the team prepared.

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:45.231
Much of it,

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:46.533
which focused on social media's

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:48.334
role in the run up to January six,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.436
did not make it into the final report.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.273
This is what did not make us into

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:54.574
the final costs.

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:55.175
Social media

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:56.910
companies failed to anticipate

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.578
post-election violence.

00:02:58.578 --> 00:02:59.045
Social media

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:00.647
platforms had a delayed response

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:03.016
that a rise of far-Right extremism.

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:04.150
Twitter was part

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.419
fazed by a fear of political reprisals.

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:07.921
Key decisions at Twitter

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:09.422
were bungled by incompetence

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:10.790
and poor judgment.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.592
I risked a lot

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:13.960
to come forward

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.262
and to speak to the committee

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.364
and to share the truth

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:19.966
about these momentous

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:21.968
occasions in history.

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:23.570
And I think it is really

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.672
a missed opportunity

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:26.406
that the committee

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:28.741
did not include the information

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.945
for front and center within their report.

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:35.014
Anika and others say the January

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:35.949
six committee

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:38.151
missed a real opportunity here.

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:39.118
You worked on the committee.

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.119
Do you agree with that?

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:42.021
The report did its job exceedingly well,

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:44.591
which was to show the American public

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:46.492
the dangers posed by

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:48.828
President Trump's

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.830
multilayered attack on our democracy.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.098
As for the draft document,

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:54.234
Jacob Glick says it includes errors

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:55.635
and shouldn't have been released.

00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:57.837
Do you think social media companies

00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:59.539
fully appreciate

00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:02.942
the role that they played in January 6th?

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:05.411
I don't think so.

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:07.780
That lack of

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.984
awareness of responsibility is stark.

00:04:10.984 --> 00:04:12.885
By seeing this information

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:15.054
we will be able to understand better

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:17.390
what happened on January six

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:18.958
in order to ensure

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:19.525
that it doesn't

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:22.695
continue to repeat itself.

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:24.831
And social media companies like Twitter

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:25.098
have gone

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:25.798
to great lengths

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:26.733
over the years to explain

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:27.233
what they say

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:28.668
are doing to crack down on violent

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.136
rhetoric on their platforms,

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.304
including, of course, in the run

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:32.538
up to January 6.

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:33.239
Now, in response

00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:34.407
to these whistleblower complaint,

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:35.875
some sources told us, look,

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.111
not everything the committee learned

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:39.779
could fit into that final report.

